Hold a definition's formals as TypeParameter records in its DracoAspect; a TypeName carries actuals only

Dev accepted, on 2026-10-21, "header formals as TypeParameter records in DracoAspect.typeParameters,
with TypeName.typeParameters carrying actual arguments only and a definition's own name keyed
bare." This closes the formal/actual question #51 had held since Codex raised it before the
ontology detour: one slot did two jobs, a header's formals and a reference's actuals, told apart
by context.

DracoAspect gains `typeParameters [TypeParameter]` as its first slot, the record a method's formals
already use — a name with optional upper and lower bounds as type-form trees — and `isEmpty` counts
it. The parser reads a header's parameters as formals (`T`, `T <: Product`, through the method-formal
reader, duplicates refused) into the aspect, and the definition's own TypeName carries none; the
implicit factory result applies the name to the formals' names, `Domain(T)`. DracoAspectOf, the
definition-backed header transform, spells the formals from the aspect — name and bounds through its
form callback — and its implicit factory result from them, so the drake surface is unchanged:
`type Coordinate(T <: Product)`. The engine reads formals from the aspect wherever it rendered the
definition's own name — the trait header, the wildcard self-type, the Null instance's variables and
instantiation, the factory's bracket, the family's parameterized test — through formalScala and
formalsBracket; a reference's parameters are still spelled from its TypeName. TypeLoader's rooting
rebuild and the generator's target-type and family-merge rebuilds carry the slot (§6's rule about
rebuilds, which cost one blind regeneration before the loader was recompiled first). NameResolution
binds the formals' names over the whole definition from the aspect and walks their bounds as
formals. The 23 parameterized trios are migrated by re-parsing their drake: their JSON names are
bare and their aspects hold the records; every generated Scala file is byte-identical except the
three trios that define the change (DracoAspect, TypeLoader, DracoAspectOf). The rest of the corpus
needed no sweep: a swept file's record is untouched.

Tests move their pins to the new shape: DrakeParseTest's header test and its round-trip helper
(formal records are not type forms), NameResolutionTest's two formal binders, DracoAspectTest's two
header fixtures (a header formal is a name with a bound, never a type form; references keep nested
parameters) and SelfDeclarationRulesTest's corpus case. Identity does not move: a definition is
keyed bare, as Uniqueness, self-declaration and the ontology already keyed it.

Probe on the compiled engine: the 23 trios re-parsed, every definition-backed Scala regenerated
through the scoped generator and byte-identical but for the three, unscoped equal to scoped equal
to pinned over all 134 definitions, the whole main and mods tree compiled, and every discovered
suite run: 849 passed, zero failed or aborted, every headline unchanged, pending mods actors 0.
No test or corpus type added.

